<Base title="About" description="I'm Chiko Mukwenha, a product engineer and product leader from Zimbabwe. A little about building software, leading projects and caring how people use them.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      I'm Chiko Mukwenha, a product engineer and product leader from Zimbabwe.
      I taught myself to code because I wanted to know how things worked, and
      somewhere along the way that grew into wanting to have a say in what we
      were making too. I enjoy writing software, a lot, but I've always been
      interested in what happens after someone starts using it. Does it make
      their work easier? Did the change we were so excited about actually help?
    </p>

    <p>
      I started out building websites with Drupal, eventually writing my own modules
      and themes, although I remember being bothered by how much I could do
      without fully understanding why the underlying software was designed the
      way it was. Joining
      <a href="/writing/2019-07-20-leaving-awesome-onesheep/">OneSheep</a>
      and working with people who pushed me to understand those things, changed
      a great deal about how I approached the work. Over three years I went from
      joining as a junior/intermediate developer to architecting and leading
      projects myself. Being trusted with a client's product, and having to
      explain the decisions behind it, gives you quite a reason to get better!
    </p>

    <p>
      Later, at <a href="/writing/2021-09-18-change/">Customer.io</a>, I worked on
      a product used by millions of people. Part of what drew me there was the
      chance to be closer to the impact of my work, whether I was changing the
      functionality, the words or something about how it looked. I think it's
      easy to underestimate how much those things belong together. A feature
      can work exactly as we intended and still leave the person using it confused,
      which is a problem I want to be involved in solving too.
    </p>

    <p>
      Currently, I'm building <a href="https://usehydra.co.za" target="_blank">Hydra</a>,
      helping South African businesses bring customers back. There are rewards,
      joining through WhatsApp and the tools a merchant needs to run things, but
      a lot of the decisions come down to fairly ordinary moments. What should
      that first message say? What does a staff member need to see? How much
      should we take on now, and what can wait? I'm writing the software and
      working through those questions, whilst also talking to business owners
      about where Hydra fits into their day.
    </p>

    <p>
      I write and speak on product engineering and customer experience, and this
      blog is where a lot of that thinking ends up. I've written about
      <a href="/writing/2019-02-09-i-agree-with-boring/">doing the boring work well</a>,
      <a href="/writing/taste/">taste in product development</a> and the things
      I've learnt from trying to get my own ideas off the ground. There are also
      experiments like taking cold showers for a month, because apparently I
      can't leave those questions alone either. For what I'm focused on at the
      moment, there's my <a href="/now/">now page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Work with me</h2>
    <p>
      I'm interested in work where I can take responsibility for how a product
      turns out, whether that's as a product engineer building with your team or
      a head of product deciding where we should put our effort and helping the
      team get there. I like being close enough to the code to understand what
      a decision will involve, and close enough to the people using it to question
      whether it's the right decision at all.
      <a href="mailto:hi@chikomukwenha.co">Tell me what you're working on</a>,
      or <a href="mailto:hi@chikomukwenha.co?subject=Speaking%20enquiry">get in touch about a talk</a>.
    </p>

